1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:07,089
Why multi-stage builds?

2
00:00:07,633 --> 00:00:15,873
Building an application needs compilers, package managers and source code; running it usually needs only the result.

3
00:00:15,873 --> 00:00:20,420
A single-stage image ships both (lesson 045 measured the waste).

4
00:00:20,420 --> 00:00:24,327
A multi-stage Dockerfile has several FROM instructions:

5
00:00:24,327 --> 00:00:32,638
each starts a new stage, and a later stage copies only the files it needs from an earlier one with COPY --from=STAGE.

6
00:00:34,400 --> 00:00:38,359
First, a fresh practice folder for this lesson.

7
00:00:38,900 --> 00:00:44,065
Build the single-stage and the multi-stage version of the same program:

8
00:00:44,065 --> 00:00:46,975
The same binary, a fraction of the size.

9
00:00:46,975 --> 00:00:48,284
It works the same:

10
00:00:48,833 --> 00:00:52,502
The same binary, a fraction of the size.

11
00:00:52,502 --> 00:00:54,153
It works the same:

12
00:00:54,700 --> 00:00:57,914
But the Go toolchain is gone from the image:

13
00:00:59,667 --> 00:01:01,175
Let me try something.

14
00:01:01,175 --> 00:01:06,991
Dockerfile.broken copies the binary from the path where a developer thinks it is:

15
00:01:07,533 --> 00:01:08,590
"/src/go-api":

16
00:01:08,590 --> 00:01:09,345
not found:

17
00:01:09,345 --> 00:01:15,383
the build stage succeeded, but there is no file at that path in the build stage.

18
00:01:15,383 --> 00:01:19,912
COPY --from paths are paths inside that stage's file system,

19
00:01:19,912 --> 00:01:23,686
not on your computer and not in the build context.

20
00:01:23,686 --> 00:01:26,403
Build the stage on its own and look:

21
00:01:28,167 --> 00:01:29,966
Here is the fix.

22
00:01:31,700 --> 00:01:32,413
Got it.

23
00:01:32,413 --> 00:01:35,164
What do I need to remember?

24
00:01:35,700 --> 00:01:40,829
Several FROMs = several stages; only the last one is the image.

25
00:01:41,367 --> 00:01:45,654
COPY --from=STAGE copies files from a stage's file system:

26
00:01:45,654 --> 00:01:48,611
build tools never reach the final image.

27
00:01:49,167 --> 00:01:51,907
--target STAGE builds up to a stage:

28
00:01:51,907 --> 00:01:55,256
for debugging, tests or a development image.

29
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:01,632
I keep hearing about multi-stage builds for Node.js.

30
00:02:01,632 --> 00:02:03,324
What is it, really?

31
00:02:03,867 --> 00:02:08,326
A Node.js service written in TypeScript (or with a bundler,

32
00:02:08,326 --> 00:02:13,920
a frontend framework, test tools) needs development dependencies to build,

33
00:02:13,920 --> 00:02:19,135
and only production dependencies plus the compiled JavaScript to run.

34
00:02:19,135 --> 00:02:27,450
A two-stage Dockerfile installs everything and compiles in the first stage, then starts the final stage fresh:

35
00:02:27,450 --> 00:02:31,229
npm ci --omit=dev and COPY --from=build /app/dist.

36
00:02:31,229 --> 00:02:37,881
The TypeScript compiler, the type definitions and the .ts sources never reach the image.

37
00:02:39,633 --> 00:02:43,593
First, a fresh practice folder for this lesson.

38
00:02:44,133 --> 00:02:47,505
Build the single-stage and the multi-stage version:

39
00:02:47,505 --> 00:02:48,629
Look inside both:

40
00:02:48,629 --> 00:02:55,043
the single-stage image carries the compiler and the sources, the multi-stage one only the output:

41
00:02:55,600 --> 00:02:56,782
Look inside both:

42
00:02:56,782 --> 00:03:03,530
the single-stage image carries the compiler and the sources, the multi-stage one only the output:

43
00:03:04,067 --> 00:03:05,666
Let's run it.

44
00:03:07,400 --> 00:03:09,023
Let me try something.

45
00:03:09,023 --> 00:03:14,434
Dockerfile.broken is the multi-stage Dockerfile with one line missing.

46
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:19,170
The build cannot catch a missing file in the final stage:

47
00:03:19,170 --> 00:03:21,219
every instruction succeeded.

48
00:03:21,219 --> 00:03:22,974
The container's log can:

49
00:03:24,733 --> 00:03:34,611
Restore the COPY --from=build /app/dist ./dist line (it is in Dockerfile) and check that the image really starts.

50
00:03:34,611 --> 00:03:40,468
A quick smoke test after every build catches this class of mistake:

51
00:03:42,233 --> 00:03:42,947
Got it.

52
00:03:42,947 --> 00:03:45,698
What do I need to remember?

53
00:03:46,233 --> 00:03:47,270
Build stage:

54
00:03:47,270 --> 00:03:50,725
all dependencies + compile; final stage:

55
00:03:50,725 --> 00:03:54,353
production dependencies + compiled output.

56
00:03:54,900 --> 00:04:00,424
npm ci --omit=dev in the final stage keeps development tools out.

57
00:04:00,967 --> 00:04:04,823
A missing COPY --from builds fine and fails at run time:

58
00:04:04,823 --> 00:04:07,096
smoke-test every image you build.

59
00:04:08,833 --> 00:04:11,913
How does multi-stage builds for Go work?

60
00:04:12,467 --> 00:04:21,979
A static Go binary needs no runtime, no shell and no C library, so its final stage can be almost empty.

61
00:04:21,979 --> 00:04:31,399
Distroless images (gcr.io/distroless/static-debian12) contain only what such a program may still need:

62
00:04:31,399 --> 00:04:38,418
CA certificates for HTTPS, time zone data, /etc/passwd with a non-root user.

63
00:04:38,418 --> 00:04:41,096
No shell, no package manager.

64
00:04:42,833 --> 00:04:46,793
First, a fresh practice folder for this lesson.

65
00:04:47,333 --> 00:04:48,933
Let's run it.

66
00:04:49,467 --> 00:04:51,066
Let's run it.

67
00:04:51,600 --> 00:04:57,144
The image's configuration shows the non-root user it inherited from the :nonroot base:

68
00:04:58,900 --> 00:05:00,698
Let me try something.

69
00:05:00,698 --> 00:05:09,175
Dockerfile.broken writes the entrypoint in shell form (ENTRYPOINT /go-api), as many Dockerfiles do:

70
00:05:09,733 --> 00:05:10,272
exec:

71
00:05:10,272 --> 00:05:11,350
"/bin/sh":

72
00:05:11,350 --> 00:05:12,751
stat /bin/sh:

73
00:05:12,751 --> 00:05:15,552
no such file or directory:

74
00:05:15,552 --> 00:05:20,186
Docker tried to start /bin/sh, not /go-api.

75
00:05:20,186 --> 00:05:30,747
The shell form ENTRYPOINT /go-api means "run /bin/sh -c "/go-api"", and distroless has no /bin/sh.

76
00:05:30,747 --> 00:05:34,303
The image configuration shows it:

77
00:05:36,067 --> 00:05:41,126
Use the exec form, a JSON array, as Dockerfile does:

78
00:05:42,867 --> 00:05:43,580
Got it.

79
00:05:43,580 --> 00:05:46,331
What do I need to remember?

80
00:05:46,867 --> 00:05:51,738
Static Go binaries run on distroless/static (or scratch):

81
00:05:51,738 --> 00:05:53,361
images of a few MB.

82
00:05:53,900 --> 00:06:00,789
:nonroot distroless images run as uid 65532 by default.

83
00:06:01,333 --> 00:06:02,269
No shell:

84
00:06:02,269 --> 00:06:11,313
ENTRYPOINT/CMD must use the exec form (JSON array), and docker exec … sh is impossible.

85
00:06:13,067 --> 00:06:16,266
Why would I need multi-stage builds for Java?

86
00:06:16,800 --> 00:06:26,589
Java needs the JDK (compiler, jar, Maven or Gradle) to build and only the JRE (the JVM and the class libraries) to run.

87
00:06:26,589 --> 00:06:35,391
A two-stage Dockerfile compiles in eclipse-temurin:25-jdk-alpine and runs in eclipse-temurin:25-jre-alpine,

88
00:06:35,391 --> 00:06:37,119
copying just the jar.

89
00:06:37,119 --> 00:06:45,509
One step further, jlink builds a custom runtime containing only the Java modules the application uses.

90
00:06:47,267 --> 00:06:51,226
First, a fresh practice folder for this lesson.

91
00:06:51,767 --> 00:07:00,536
Build the single-stage image of lesson 043 (Dockerfile.single) for comparison, and the multi-stage one:

92
00:07:01,100 --> 00:07:02,699
